    "created_at": "2026-11-09T15:05:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-09T15:05:35Z",
    "body": "> @noamr I got some pushback on storing the registry on the definition and since this is an edge I decided to change the approach. This does mean that Chromium does it incorrectly however. WDYT?\r\n\r\nLGTM remains. Given how obscure this edge case is, avoiding the extra reference on `custom element definition` and matching Gecko and WebKit seems fine.\r\n\r\n<details><summary>A couple of minor quirks with this approach just for the record, found by AI</summary>\r\n\r\n- Adopting into a document with a scoped registry (`documentRegistry.initialize(contentDocument)`) or a `null` registry before upgrade succeeds (because `adopt` sets `element`'s registry to `null`, and removing `active custom element constructor map[C]` makes `[HTMLConstructor]` fall back to `current global object`'s `Document`'s registry as if `new C()` were called directly), whereas adopting into a document with another global registry fails with `TypeError`.\r\n\r\n- If the same constructor `C` happens to be registered in both the original global registry (`definition1`) and the target document's global registry (`definition2`), `[HTMLConstructor]` will find `definition2` (whose `construction stack` is empty), allocate a new element via the `new C()` branch, and then fail the `SameValue(constructResult, element)` check in `upgrade an element` with a `TypeError`.\r\n</details>\r\n\r\nOverall though I'm fine with this direction.",
